import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap

meta = {
	title: "Tooling",
	description: "rocx build, check, lint, routes and fmt; compiler errors reported at your .rocx lines; setting up an app.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "commands", label: "Commands" },
		{ id: "errors", label: "Errors at your lines" },
		{ id: "stories", label: "Stories" },
		{ id: "setup", label: "Setting up an app" },
		{ id: "repository", label: "This repository" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Tooling"
				title="Three commands, and the compiler you already have"
			>
				rocx is a transpiler with no dependencies: one file of JavaScript turns markup into Roc, and the
				Roc compiler does the rest. The tooling is about keeping that invisible.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="commands">Commands</Heading>
			<div class="commands">
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx build {"<dir>…"}<span>--client {"<out>"}</span></p>
					<div>
						<p>
							Each <code>.rocx</code> under the directories becomes the <code>.roc</code> beside it: a page's
							markup (<code>import html.Html</code>) rendered once, a component's (<code>import pf.Html</code>)
							with computations and handlers. An app's <code>routes/</code> become
							<code>Generated/Routes.roc</code>, <code>Paths.roc</code>, with endpoints, <code>Api.roc</code>,
							and <code>Generated/App.roc</code>, the program its
							<code>main.roc</code> gives what only it can say (<code>App.program</code>, with the jobs the app
							defines and its <code>migrations/*.sql</code>). Route files that are components
							(<code>import pf.Html</code>, <code>view!</code>) also get <code>Generated/Root.roc</code>, the page
							at where it is in its layouts.
							<code>--client</code> also writes the browser's copy of the app, where an endpoint in
							<code>Api</code> is only its method and URL, a route file has no <code>middleware</code>, and
							the imports only those used are gone; for components,
							its <code>main.roc</code> and <code>static/rocx/</code>, where start.js loads the runtime and wasm.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx check -- {"<command>"}</p>
					<p>
						Runs a command (<code>roc check</code>, <code>roc build</code>) with its reports pointed into
						the <code>.rocx</code> files: <a href="#errors">errors at your lines</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx lint {"<dir>…"}</p>
					<p>
						Refuses <code>Html.source_text</code> in hand-written <code>.roc</code> files, where it would
						put any string in a page as markup (<a href={"${Paths.safety}#lint"}>why</a>).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx routes {"<dir>"}</p>
					<p>
						Lists an app's routes: each method a path answers, the file that answers it, and the
						middleware that file runs behind, after the app's default
						(<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>what runs, in what order</a>).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx fmt {"<path>…"}<span>--check</span></p>
					<p>
						Formats the Roc in each <code>.rocx</code> file, and each <code>.roc</code> file rocx didn't
						write, with <code>roc fmt</code>. Markup, and the Roc in its holes, stay as written: a piece
						of markup moves only with the code around it, and a <code>{"<pre>"}</code>'s text never moves.
						<code>--check</code> only says which files aren't formatted.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p>
				<code>build</code> writes a file only when its content changes, so a watcher that sees it doesn't
				build forever; deletes the <code>.roc</code> files it wrote whose <code>.rocx</code> is gone; and stops
				at the first markup problem with its line and column. Generated files end with a
				<code># rocx: generated from …</code> line, and stay out of version control.
			</p>

			<Heading id="errors">Errors at your lines</Heading>
			<p>
				Because line <var>N</var> of the generated file is line <var>N</var> of yours, the compiler's reports
				already name the right line. <code>rocx check</code> makes them read as if the compiler had seen
				your file: it rewrites the location, quotes your line instead of the generated one, and moves the
				caret onto the same code. Here is a mistake, a number where text belongs:
			</p>
			<Code title="Posts.rocx" source={Sources.check_source} />
			<p>and what <code>rocx check -- roc check Posts.roc</code> printed when this page was built:</p>
			<div class="report"><Code title="The compiler, through rocx check" source={Sources.check_report} numbers={Bool.False} plain /></div>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="The fix">
				<p>
					<code>{"{posts.len().to_str()}"}</code>: a text hole takes a <code>Str</code>, and Roc never turns
					a number into one by itself.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<p>
				It adds what the compiler can't know about the platform. A report that the builtin <code>Try</code> has
				no <code>encoder_for</code> or <code>parser_for</code>, met when an endpoint takes or answers one,
				gets a hint after it: a <code>Try</code> can't go as JSON on this Roc, and a tag union,
				<code>{"[Ok(a), Err(e)]"}</code>, can.
			</p>

			<Heading id="stories">Stories</Heading>
			<p>
				A component's stories are the ways it looks, written beside it: a <code>{"<story name=\"…\">"}</code>
				in its block, among its definitions, holds the markup of one. Each becomes a definition in its
				place (<code>story_tip</code>), so every build checks it against the component, and only a dev
				build calls it. With <code>just dev</code> (<code>rocx build --dev</code>), the server has a
				catalog of them at <code>/__rocx/stories</code>: every story down the side, which folds away
				(<kbd>S</kbd>); the one picked in a frame, with the line it is written at, which can fill the
				window (<kbd>F</kbd>, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> to come back); and a component's stories side by side.
				A component's story is the app's page at its path, which the browser hydrates; one of
				<code>html.Html</code> markup is shown in the front page's layouts, which can tell it by its
				path (<code>/__rocx/stories/…</code>): this site's shows it on its own. The site's own build,
				and what <code>just site</code> publishes, has no stories at all.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ui_callout.file} source={Sources.ui_callout.rocx} />
			<p>
				A story may use the page (<code>page.request</code>, <code>page.context</code>). An app's
				<code>stories/</code> directory holds modules of stories alone: drafts of something new, each a
				component with its own <code>{"<style>"}</code>, side by side in the catalog. Only a dev build
				has it, so nothing in it is published, its styles neither. This site's
				<code>site/stories/Landing.rocx</code> has the front page as it is, and drafts of it in six
				themes.
			</p>

			<Heading id="setup">Setting up an app</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>html/</code> is a Roc package: the <code>Html</code> pages are written with. Add it to your
				app's headers, run <code>rocx build</code> before <code>roc build</code>, and <code>rocx check</code>
				instead of <code>roc check</code>. An app is two
				programs (<a href={Paths.platform_}>the platform</a>): the server's, on the server platform (native, in
				a Rust host), and the browser's, its islands, on the web platform (wasm). With pages in
				<code>routes/</code> (<a href={Paths.routing}>routing</a>), the server's app answers every request
				through the router rocx writes (the GETs it makes as a page renders too), behind its default
				middleware, and gives the platform its islands' root:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="server.roc"
				source={"app [program] {\n\tpf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\",\n\thtml: \"../html/main.roc\",\n}\n\nimport pf.Server\nimport pf.Middleware\nimport Generated/Routes\nimport Islands\n\nprogram = {\n\trespond!,\n\tmigrations: [],\n\tjobs: [],\n\tcrons: [],\n\tsignals: { main!: Islands.view! },\n}\n\nrespond! = |method, target, headers, body, facts| {\n\trequest = Server.from_http(method, target, headers, body)\n\tMiddleware.same_origin({}).run!(request, { facts: facts }, |passed, context|\n\t\tRoutes.respond!(passed, context))\n}"}
			/>
			<Code title={Sources.client_app.file} source={Sources.client_app.rocx} />
			<Code
				title="Justfile"
				source={"build:\n\tnode rocx/bin/rocx.mjs build app --client build/client\n\troc build --target=wasm32 --opt=size --specialize=yes --output=build/client.wasm build/client/client.roc\n\tnode rocx/server/build.mjs app/server.roc build/server --opt=speed\n\ncheck:\n\tnode rocx/bin/rocx.mjs lint app\n\tnode rocx/bin/rocx.mjs check -- roc check app/server.roc"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain
			/>
			<p>
				Pages alone need no particular platform: the <code>html</code> package renders strings on any.
				Requests, routes, endpoints, events and middleware are both platforms' (<code>pf.Server</code>,
				<code>pf.Endpoint</code>, <code>pf.Middleware</code>, <code>pf.Events</code>, <code>pf.OpenApi</code>).
				Islands need the <code>web/</code> platform in the browser, and a server that renders them (the
				server platform does, natively); jobs, cron, email, Postgres, live rooms and actors are the server
				platform's.
			</p>

			<Heading id="repository">This repository</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>src/transpile.mjs</code>, <code>bin/rocx.mjs</code></td><td>the transpiler and the command</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>html/</code></td><td>the package pages are written with: markup, rendering, safety</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>web/</code></td><td>the browser's platform: its Roc header, the modules both platforms have (components'; and requests, endpoints, events, middleware), the Zig host, the runtime</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>server/</code></td><td>the server's platform: its Roc header and modules, the Rust host, the build</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>site/</code></td><td>this site: its pages in <code>routes/</code>, in rocx; <code>build.mjs</code> builds them, <code>serve.mjs</code> serves them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>spike/</code></td><td>experiments: <code>spike/suspense</code>, how a read that has to wait can stop a computation in wasm</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>test/</code>, <code>web/test/</code>, <code>site/test/</code>, <code>server/test/</code></td><td>the transpiler, the browser's platform, the site in a real browser, the server's platform</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Zig 0.17.1 and Rust ≥1.85 from your system (e.g. Nix), or <code>just setup</code> downloads them.
				Roc is a fork of the pinned nightly with the compiler fixes rocx needs, which <code>just setup</code>
				builds into <code>.tools/</code>.
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"just setup   # the Roc fork rocx builds with, the pinned nightly, Zig and Rust if need be, into .tools/\njust db      # a Postgres in Docker, for the server platform\njust test    # everything: the transpiler, both platforms, the site in Chromium\njust dev     # this site, rendered per request, rebuilt and reloaded on save\njust site    # the static site, in site/dist"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	/* The commands, a card each: how it is written, and what it does. */
	.commands {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
		margin: var(--space-4) 0;
	}

	.command {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
		gap: var(--space-4);
		padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--bg-raised);
	}

	.command-usage {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--accent);
		font: 600 var(--size-sm) / 1.6 var(--mono);
	}

	.command-usage span {
		display: block;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-weight: 400;
	}

	.command p:not(.command-usage) {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: var(--size-sm);
	}

	.command p:not(.command-usage) + p {
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
	}

	/* The compiler's report, as it prints it: its lines aren't wrapped. */
	.report :global(pre) {
		white-space: pre;
	}

	@media (max-width: 52rem) {
		.command {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
